 "32 from 32 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The threesome of " [:a {:href "/athletes/112428"} "Emma Twigg"] " (NZL), " [:a {:href "/athletes/145506"} "Karolien Florijn"] " (NED), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/140653"} "Tara Rigney"] " (AUS) were the heavy medal favorites in women’s single sculls rowing at Paris 2024."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Twigg was the defending gold medalist from Tokyo 2020, appearing at her fifth Olympics in Paris. She won silver medals at the 2022 and 2023 World Championships and had seven medals at the Worlds in single sculls, with four silvers and two bronzes. Florijn won the World Championships in single sculls in 2022 and 2023, and won the 2023 Luzern Regatta. She won a silver medal at Tokyo 2020 in the four but switched to sculling after that regatta. Rigney rowed double sculls at the Tokyo 2020 Olympics, finishing seventh in winning the B Final alongside " [:a {:href "/athletes/140641"} "Amanda Bateman"] ". Rigney was third in single sculls at the 2022-2023 World Championships and placed second, behind Florijn, at the 2023 Luzern Regatta."] "\n\n" [:p {} "And that was almost the podium in Paris. Florijn took the lead early in the A final and held on, although she was challenged in the final 500 metres by Twigg, who took silver this time. Rigney looked to have the bronze medal but she was passed in the last 100 metres by Lithuanian " [:a {:href "/athletes/153769"} "Viktorija Senkutė"] ". This was the third ever medal in women’s rowing for Lithuania, all bronze, after the 2000 and 2016 double sculls."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Florijn’s gold medal was the first medal for the Netherlands in women’s single sculls, their best previous finish a fourth by " [:a {:href "/athletes/38829"} "Harriet van Ettekoven"] " in 1988. Twigg became only the fifth rower to win two or more medals in this event."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [: